cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >
  If you use RoughVolLab in your research, please cite it using
  the metadata below. This file is machine-readable by GitHub,
  Zenodo, and most reference managers.

title: >
  RoughVolLab: Simulation, pricing, and optimal control
  under rough stochastic volatility

abstract: >
  RoughVolLab is an open-source Python laboratory for research on rough
  stochastic volatility, built on the principle of honest measurement
  over confident numbers. Rather than assuming the rough-volatility
  paradigm, it asks whether roughness is identifiable, priceable,
  tradeable and hedgeable. One heavily tested simulation engine
  underlies the estimation, pricing, execution and hedging layers:
  Hurst-exponent estimation from realised variance; pricing of
  path-dependent options by multilevel and conditional Monte Carlo;
  execution benchmarked against the Almgren-Chriss schedule; and deep
  hedging with path-signature features and a CVaR objective. A
  characteristic-function pricer underlies the calibration layer, which
  fits the rough Heston model to option surfaces, including a live
  Deribit pipeline. Five falsifiable questions are answered with what
  the mathematics and the data yield, and four of the answers are
  negative. Beside them, a pre-registered study (P5) audits the tools
  used on the paradigm, scoring two neural calibrators against exact
  reference posteriors on an emulated rough Heston model. Significant
  design decisions are recorded with their predicted outcomes before
  the result is known, an interactive tour lets readers drive each
  result in the browser, and a plain-English guide explains the
  paradigm.

